When you get **Personal Independence Payment (PIP)**, you must tell the **Department for Work and Pensions (DWP)** straight away about certain changes. Some changes can affect how much PIP you get. Others are just updates to your details.

{% callout type="info" title="The short version" %}
- Call the **PIP enquiry line** on **0800 121 4433** as soon as something changes
- Report it if your **needs change**, or you go into **hospital**, a **care home** or **abroad for more than 4 weeks**
- Your PIP could **go up, go down, stay the same or stop**
- If you do not report a change, you may have to **pay money back**
{% /callout %}

## Changes you must report

You must contact the PIP enquiry line **straight away** if:

- you need **more or less help** with daily living or mobility tasks
- a health professional tells you your condition will last **longer or shorter** than you said before
- a doctor or nurse says you might have **12 months or less to live**. You may be able to get a higher rate under the **special rules for end of life**
- you go into a **hospital**, a **hospice**, a **nursing home** or a **care home**
- you go into a **residential school or college**
- you go into **foster care**, or into the care of a local authority or a health and social care trust
- you go to **prison** or are held in detention
- you plan to go **abroad for more than 4 weeks**
- your **immigration status** changes and you are not a British or Irish citizen
- you, or your husband, wife, civil partner or a parent you depend on, start or stop getting pensions or benefits from an **EU country, Switzerland, Norway, Iceland or Liechtenstein**

### If you are in a care home

You also need to tell the DWP if:

- the way your **care home fees are paid** changes, for example you start or stop getting money from the NHS or your council
- you were staying **temporarily** and you become a **permanent** resident
- you **move** to another care home
- you go into **hospital**

### Changes to your details

You must also report changes to your:

- **name, address or phone number**
- **bank account**
- **doctor**
- **appointee** or anyone else acting for you

These do not normally change how much PIP you get, but the DWP needs them to keep paying you.

{% callout type="warn" title="Moving to Scotland" %}
PIP is being replaced by **Adult Disability Payment** in Scotland. If you move from England or Wales to Scotland, contact the PIP enquiry line and make a new claim for Adult Disability Payment. Read about [Adult Disability Payment](/benefits/adp/).
{% /callout %}

## Things you do not need to report

PIP is **not means-tested**, so you do not need to report:

- starting or stopping work, or changing your hours
- changes to your **income or savings**
- changes to your other benefits, unless they come from one of the countries listed above

But if any of these happen **because your needs have changed**, you must report the change in your needs.

## How to report a change

Call the **PIP enquiry line**:

- **Telephone:** 0800 121 4433
- **Relay UK** (if you cannot hear or speak on the phone): 18001 then 0800 121 4433
- **British Sign Language (BSL):** use the video relay service on GOV.UK
- **Opening times:** Monday to Friday, 9am to 5pm

If you need help, you can ask for someone to be **added to your call**, or ask someone to **call for you** while you are with them.

**Tips:**

- write down the **date** you called and the **name** of the person you spoke to
- keep any **letters** about the change, such as a hospital discharge letter
- if you report by letter, keep a copy and get proof of postage

## What happens after you report a change

### If your needs have changed

The DWP may send you a form to fill in about how your condition affects you now. It may also ask for an assessment by phone, video call or in person. It works much like a [PIP assessment](/benefits/pip/assessment/).

You will then get a letter telling you what will happen to your PIP. Your award could **go up, go down, stay the same or stop**.

If your needs have got **worse**, describe what is harder now and give examples, just as you would on a new claim. Our free [PIP self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/) can help you see which activities may now score points. Nothing leaves your device.

If you disagree with the decision, you can [challenge it](/benefits/pip/challenge-a-decision/). You have **one month** from the date on the letter.

### If you go into hospital or a care home

- If you are aged **18 or over** and go into an **NHS hospital**, your PIP usually **stops after 28 days**. Stays that are 28 days or less apart are added together.
- If you go into a **care home** paid for by your council or the NHS, the **daily living** part usually stops after **28 days**. The **mobility** part usually carries on.
- If you **pay for your care home yourself**, your PIP usually carries on.

Your PIP should start again when you leave, as long as you tell the DWP.

### If you go abroad

You can usually keep getting PIP for up to **13 weeks** while you are abroad, or up to **26 weeks** if you are going for **medical treatment** for your condition. Tell the DWP before you go if it will be more than **4 weeks**.

## Reviews of your PIP award

A change of circumstances is different from a **planned review**. Your award letter tells you when your PIP will end and whether it will be reviewed.

For a review, the DWP sends you a form called **Personal Independence Payment Review**. You usually have **1 month** to send it back. You keep getting PIP while the review happens. If you need more time, call the PIP enquiry line before the deadline.

## If you have been paid too much

You may have to **pay money back** if you:

- did **not report** a change straight away
- gave **wrong information**
- were paid too much **by mistake**

You could also be **taken to court** or have to **pay a penalty** if you give wrong information or do not report a change on purpose.

If the DWP says you owe money, you can ask how it was worked out and challenge the decision if you think it is wrong. You can also ask to pay it back in **smaller amounts** you can afford. Get free help from Citizens Advice, Turn2us or a local welfare rights service.

{% callout type="good" title="Not sure if you need to report something?" %}
If in doubt, report it. It is much easier to sort out a change straight away than to deal with a debt later. You can also get free, independent advice from Citizens Advice or a local welfare rights service first.
{% /callout %}
